# vsqlite++ (unreleased)

- Writable databases restored via `sqlite::deserialize` can now grow beyond the size of
  the input image (`SQLITE_DESERIALIZE_RESIZEABLE`); previously inserts reported
  `SQLITE_FULL` once the image size was exhausted. Read-only deserialization stays
  immutable.
- Fixed statements returned to the statement cache while their cursor was still on a
  row keeping the implicit read transaction open, which blocked writers on other
  connections until the cache was cleared. Statements are now reset and their bindings
  cleared before being retained, statements that fail to reset are finalized instead of
  cached, and statement cleanup no longer lets exceptions escape through destructors.
- Fixed `sqlite::result::reset()` only clearing the end flag without rewinding the underlying
  prepared statement, which made a partially consumed cursor continue from the next row instead
  of restarting from the first row. Reset errors of the last evaluation are now reported with a
  `sqlite::database_exception`, parameter bindings are preserved, and results sharing the same
  statement are rewound and revived together, including exhausted ones.
- Fixed named in-memory database URIs (`file:name?mode=memory&cache=shared`) being
  created as ordinary files on disk instead of living in memory.
- Database names starting with `file:` are now interpreted as SQLite URIs: path
  validation uses the decoded URI path and the `mode=memory` query parameter is
  matched strictly. All other names keep being treated as literal filenames.
- Fixed a crash (SIGSEGV) when applying a changeset or patchset containing conflicting changes:
  the null conflict handler passed to `sqlite3changeset_apply` is replaced by a default abort
  policy, so conflicts now raise `sqlite::database_exception` and roll back partial application.
- Added conflict resolution support to `sqlite::apply_changeset` and `sqlite::apply_patchset`:
  a fixed `conflict_policy` (`omit`, `replace`, `abort`) or a custom `conflict_handler` receiving
  a `changeset_conflict` descriptor. Exceptions thrown by a handler are caught at the SQLite C
  boundary, abort the application, and are rethrown to the caller. The fixed `replace` policy
  omits conflicts that SQLite does not allow replacing (missing rows, constraint and foreign
  key violations) instead of failing the application with `SQLITE_MISUSE`.

# vsqlite++ 0.4.2

- Fixed a statement ownership regression in 0.4.1 that finalized prepared statements immediately
  after preparation and caused test/runtime crashes.

# vsqlite++ 0.4.1

- Hardened `sqlite::result` and `sqlite::connection_pool` lifetime handling.
- Fixed command clearing to remove retained SQLite parameter bindings.
- Made expression column declared-type lookup return an empty string when SQLite reports no type.
- Improved bundled SQLite build/test behavior and snapshot test robustness.
